Kubernetes and OpenShift Summary
1. Resources and Pods
In Kubernetes and Red Hat OpenShift (RHOCP), many resources are used to create or manage pods.
Examples include:
•	Deployments
•	Jobs
•	Services
•	Routes
•	Ingress
Pods are the basic unit that runs containers.
________________________________________
2. Resource Creation Methods
Resources can be created in two ways:
Imperative Approach
You tell the cluster exactly what action to perform using commands.
Example:
oc create deployment myapp --image=nginx
Characteristics:
•	Quick
•	Command-based
•	Used for testing or simple tasks
________________________________________
Declarative Approach
You define the desired state in a YAML file, and the cluster ensures it matches that state.
Example:
oc apply -f deployment.yaml
Characteristics:
•	Configuration-based
•	Version control friendly
•	Used in production environments
________________________________________
3. oc new-app Command
The oc new-app command automatically creates resources.
It uses heuristics (automatic detection) to determine:
•	Image
•	Deployment
•	Service
•	Build configuration
Example:
oc new-app nginx
This command can automatically create:
•	Deployment
•	Service
•	ImageStream
________________________________________
4. Deployments
A Deployment is the main way to run applications in OpenShift.
Responsibilities:
•	Creates pods
•	Manages replicas
•	Handles updates
•	Maintains desired state
Example:
oc create deployment myapp --image=nginx
Deployment manages ReplicaSets, which manage pods.
________________________________________
5. Workload API Resources
Kubernetes provides several workload resources to create pods.
The choice depends on how the application should run.
Resource	Purpose
Deployment	Long-running applications
Job	One-time task
CronJob	Scheduled tasks
DaemonSet	One pod per node
StatefulSet	Stateful applications
________________________________________
6. Job Resource
A Job runs a task once.
Example use cases:
•	Database migrations
•	Data processing
•	Batch tasks
Example command:
oc create job db-backup --image=backup-image
Features:
•	Runs until successful
•	Retries if the task fails
•	Stops after a specified retry limit
________________________________________
7. Projects and Labels
Projects
Projects organize resources inside OpenShift.
Example:
oc project my-project
Projects help isolate:
•	Applications
•	Teams
•	Permissions
________________________________________
Labels
Labels are key-value pairs used to identify resources.
Example:
labels:
  app: frontend
  env: production
Labels are used to:
•	Select pods
•	Group resources
•	Manage services
Example:
oc get pods -l app=frontend
________________________________________
8. Services and Routes
Service
A Service provides internal network communication between pods.
Example:
oc expose deployment myapp --port=8080
Service types:
•	ClusterIP
•	NodePort
•	LoadBalancer
________________________________________
Route (OpenShift)
A Route exposes an application to external users.
It maps:
DNS hostname → Service → Pod
Example:
oc expose service myservice
Example route:
myapp.apps.cluster.example.com
________________________________________
Network Flow Example
User Browser
     │
     ▼
   Route
     │
     ▼
   Service
     │
     ▼
